# Copy to sabha.conf (gitignored) and fill in. $VAR and ~ expand; earlier keys can be used later.
SABHA_WS=~/Work/my-workspace
SABHA_IMPL=$SABHA_WS/path/to/impl
# Optional: where FOR-ME.md and FOR-ME.html are written (default: SABHA_IMPL). Keep it outside this repo.
#SABHA_FORME_DIR=$SABHA_WS/path/to/board
#SABHA_CSV_DIR=$SABHA_WS/path/to/tracker   # agents.csv and for-me.csv, written on every render
# Tracker: html (default: offline page from the CSVs, no login, tracker/build-html.py), xlsx (tracker/build-xlsx.py, import
# into any spreadsheet) or sheet (Google Sheet: the better option for phone use, sharing and the Inbox/Decisions round
# trip; one-time `bin/sabha-sheet auth`, then `bin/sabha-sheet sync` copies the CSVs, no agent needed).
#SABHA_TRACKER=html
#SABHA_SHEET_ID=
# 1 = an agent session that has the Google Sheets connector also syncs every 30 min (tracker/cron-prompts.md). Default 0
# on every plan: that is 48 agent runs a day (about 24 per 12 working hours), each a model call that spends
# subscription usage even when nothing changed (token cost per run not measured). `bin/sabha-sheet sync` on a timer
# gives the same result at zero usage; turn this on only if you cannot run the script.
#SABHA_SHEET_AGENT_SYNC=0
# The opening prompt says "You are <Name> in $SABHA_PROJECT session setup"; the relay reports to $SABHA_USER.
SABHA_PROJECT="My project"
SABHA_USER="the user"
# Terminal backend: herdr (default, every OS; install with brew install herdr). iterm = the frozen macOS iTerm2 path.
# On Windows run the scripts in Git Bash (UNTESTED). Nudge a role: bin/sabha-nudge <Name> "text". Mac/Linux: run
# `herdr attach` in a VS Code integrated terminal to view and chat with any pane.
#SABHA_BACKEND=herdr
# 1 passes --remote-control to Claude panes (Pro/Max only; an org policy can block it).
#SABHA_REMOTE=0
# Plan: the roster, panes, copies and labels of sabha.<plan>.conf beside this file (pro, max5, max20) replace the ones
# below. `sabha-up.sh --plan pro` does the same for one run. Leave it unset to use the roster written here.
#SABHA_PLAN=pro
# Roster: Name:agent:model[:effort[:flags]] entries separated by spaces (a name with a space is not supported; effort
# defaults to medium). The agent is claude, opencode, codex, agy or muse; a row without one (Name:model...) is claude,
# so older rosters still parse. Claude rows start from sabha-up.sh and sabha-run; the other agents start through herdr
# (opencode tested; codex, agy and muse UNTESTED) and sabha-run never serves them. The one roster source
# for the launchers, sabha-watch and the board. Order matters: the relay first, the dispatcher second, then the panes
# in tab order (each tab: top row left to right, then the bottom row, up to 8).
SABHA_ROSTER="Vidur:claude:opus:medium Yudhishthira:claude:opus:medium Bhishma:claude:fable:medium Bhima:claude:opus:high Arjun:claude:opus:medium Sahadev:claude:sonnet:high Nakul:claude:sonnet:medium Karna:claude:sonnet:medium Sanjaya:claude:haiku:high Duryodhan:claude:opus:high:gate,lazy Dushasan:claude:sonnet:high:gate,lazy Shakuni:claude:opus:high:gate,lazy Draupadi:claude:sonnet:high:gate,lazy Gandhari:claude:sonnet:medium:gate,lazy VedVyasa:claude:sonnet:medium:gate,lazy Barbarika:claude:haiku:medium:gate Abhimanyu:claude:haiku:medium:gate Krishna:claude:opus:medium:ondemand Ashwatthama:claude:haiku:high:ondemand Dronacharya:claude:sonnet:medium:ondemand"
# Flags (comma list): ondemand = no pane, start it with sabha-up.sh <Name>; gate = a pane in the second tab (Gate);
# lazy = no pane; bin/sabha-run starts it headless when a brief lands; mcp = keeps the user's MCP servers. No flag = a
# Build-tab pane.
# Panes: when set, exactly these roles get a pane, in this order (the relay first, so it restarts like the rest);
# every other profile is on demand: a subagent of the dispatcher with its own model and effort, or sabha-up.sh <Name>.
# When unset, the flags above decide.
#SABHA_PANES="Vidur Yudhishthira Bhima Arjun Nakul"
# Copies: Arjun-1, Arjun-2 ... take Arjun's profile with their own inbox, status file and worktree; at most this many
# per role (2 on Pro, 4 on Max).
#SABHA_MAX_COPIES=2
# Role labels, shown as "Bhishma (Architect)" in the opening prompt and on the board.
#SABHA_LABEL_Bhishma="Architect"
# A role id is one word (Ved Vyasa is VedVyasa). SABHA_PROMPT_<Name> replaces the
# opening prompt after "then status/<name>.md, " (no double quotes, $ or backticks after expansion).
SABHA_PROMPT_Krishna="then ask the user which topic to practise. You are the on-demand tutor: one open question at a time, read-only on code, no briefs. Report to the user directly."
# Headless runner (bin/sabha-run, docs/headless-runner.md). Which roles it serves: lazy (default), all, or names.
#SABHA_RUN_ROLES=lazy
# At most this many runs at once (default 2); a run is stopped after SABHA_RUN_TIMEOUT seconds (default 3600).
#SABHA_RUN_MAX=2
#SABHA_RUN_TIMEOUT=3600
# One log per run (default $SABHA_IMPL/runs). SABHA_RUN_AUTO=1 makes each sabha-watch run end with a sabha-run pass.
#SABHA_RUN_LOGS=$SABHA_IMPL/runs
#SABHA_RUN_AUTO=1
# Pane supervisor (bin/sabha-loop <Name>) and usage alerts (bin/statusline-sabha.sh, bin/sabha-limits.sh).
# Claude panes restart fresh at SABHA_LOOP_LIMIT percent context once the handoff is saved; a context file older
# than SABHA_LOOP_STALE seconds only warns. SABHA_NOTIFY lists the usage percentages (5-hour and 7-day windows) that
# fire a notification, each once per window. At SABHA_STOP percent no new session starts until the window resets
# (0 or off disables). SABHA_PUSH=ntfy also sends each alert to the ntfy topic (public topics are readable by name).
#SABHA_LOOP_LIMIT=30
#SABHA_LOOP_STALE=120
#SABHA_NOTIFY="10 20 30 40 50 60 70 80 90"
#SABHA_STOP=90
#SABHA_PUSH=off
#SABHA_NTFY_TOPIC=
#SABHA_NTFY_URL=https://ntfy.sh
SABHA_WAITING="Reviewer1 Reviewer2"
# Optional: "Name=task text;Name=task text" for a role whose status file has no Current: or Idle: line.
#SABHA_TASK_FALLBACK="Name=task text"

# Example question item for for-me.items (blocks are separated by a line that is exactly ---). Keys: ask, q, explain,
# opta..opth ("option text | consequence | example"), rec. Plain items need none of them.
#---
#id: 99
#utc: 2026-10-14T09:00:00Z
#owner: Vidur
#title: Which branch gets the demo
#ask: Pat
#q: Which branch should the demo be built from?
#explain: The demo freezes whatever the branch holds, so a late change would not show.
#opta: develop | newest code, may be unstable | includes yesterday's unreviewed merge
#optb: release/1.2 | stable, a day old | what Alex already reviewed
#rec: b
